<main id="main">
<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg" data-parallax="0.15"><img src="/assets/img/photos/clipboard.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/clipboard-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/clipboard.webp 960w" sizes="100vw" width="960" height="640" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li aria-current="page">Appraisal roadmap</li></ol></nav><p class="kicker">Process guide</p><h1>The Mississippi Insurance Appraisal Roadmap</h1><p class="lede">8 stages, from the first disagreement to payment after the award — what happens, who does what, and where files go wrong.</p><div class="btns"><a class="btn btn-gold" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/talk-to-an-appraiser/">Free claim review</a></div><ul class="hero-facts"><li><small>Stages</small><b>8</b></li><li><small>Panel</small><b>2 appraisers + umpire</b></li><li><small>Award</small><b>Any 2 of 3 sign</b></li></ul></div></section><div class="wrap cols"><article class="prose"><p>Appraisal is the part of your property policy that lets you and your carrier settle a disagreement about <strong>how much</strong> a covered loss is worth without going to court. It doesn’t decide whether the damage is covered, and it doesn’t replace a lawyer. It is a structured way to put two estimates side by side and get a binding number on the amount of loss.</p><p>The roadmap below walks through the eight stages most Mississippi appraisals pass through, from the groundwork you lay before sending a demand to the check that arrives after the award. Every policy words its appraisal clause a little differently, so treat the timeframes here as common patterns and let your own policy language control.</p><div class="road"><section class="road-step"><h2><span>1</span>Confirm coverage and gather the file</h2><p>Appraisal works well when the only real argument is price. Before you demand it, read the carrier’s letters closely. If the carrier has accepted the claim but paid less than your contractor’s number, you likely have an amount-of-loss dispute. If it has denied the claim outright, or says the damage came from an excluded cause, that is a coverage question appraisal can’t settle on its own.</p><p>Then pull the file together:</p><ul><li>The full policy, including the declarations page and every endorsement</li><li>The carrier’s estimate, payment letters and any engineer or adjuster reports</li><li>Your own photos, videos, receipts and contractor estimates</li><li>A simple timeline of the storm, the reporting date and each inspection</li></ul><p>Our <a href="/claim-calculator-suite/appraisal-readiness-checkup/">appraisal readiness checkup</a> can help you spot gaps before the clock starts.</p></section><section class="road-step"><h2><span>2</span>Send a written appraisal demand</h2><p>Most appraisal clauses start with a written demand from either side. The letter doesn’t need to be long. It usually identifies the policy and claim numbers, states that you and the carrier disagree on the amount of loss, invokes the appraisal provision, and names your appraiser or says you will name one within the policy’s deadline.</p><p>Send it in a way you can prove later, such as tracked mail plus email, and keep a copy. Some policies set conditions first, such as a signed proof of loss or a request for an examination under oath. Read your clause for those steps.</p><p>A demand doesn’t end the claim or your other rights, but deadlines in your policy and under Mississippi law keep running. If you are unsure how they apply to you, confirm with a Mississippi attorney.</p></section><section class="road-step"><h2><span>3</span>Each side names its appraiser</h2><p>After the demand, each side picks its own appraiser. Many policies give both parties a set window to do this, often 20 days from the written demand, but the policy controls and some forms differ. Write the date of your demand on your calendar and count forward.</p><p>The clause usually calls for a “competent” and “disinterested” or “independent” appraiser. In plain terms, your appraiser should understand construction pricing and should not have a stake in the outcome that the policy forbids. Fee terms that hinge on the size of the award can draw objections, so ask about the fee structure up front and get it in writing.</p><p>You pay your appraiser, and the carrier pays its own. See how our <a href="/our-appraisal-services/appraisal-demand-representation/">appraisal demand representation</a> works.</p></section><section class="road-step"><h2><span>4</span>The appraisers choose an umpire</h2><p>Once both appraisers are named, they try to agree on an umpire: a neutral third person who steps in only on items the two appraisers can’t resolve. Many clauses give them a window of often 15 days to agree. If they can’t, most policies let either side ask a court of record to appoint one.</p><p>A good umpire candidate knows Mississippi building costs and has no ties to either side. The appraisers usually exchange a short list, check for conflicts, and confirm the umpire’s rate and availability in writing before work starts.</p><p>The umpire’s fee is often split evenly between you and the carrier, though your policy may say otherwise. We also serve as a <a href="/our-appraisal-services/neutral-umpire-services/">neutral umpire</a> on files where we represent neither side.</p></section><section class="road-step"><h2><span>5</span>Inspections and competing estimates</h2><p>Next, each appraiser inspects the property and builds or reviews an estimate. On a roof that may mean measuring every slope, counting layers, checking pitch for steep charges and documenting ridge, valley and flashing details. Inside, it means tracing water stains, testing for moisture and listing every room that needs work.</p><p>Both appraisers can attend the same walkthrough, which often saves time and narrows disputes early. Be ready to give access to the attic, crawlspace and outbuildings, and to share repair invoices for any emergency work already done.</p><p>The appraisers then trade estimates. Most use the same line-item estimating software carriers use, so the two documents can be compared item by item rather than as two unrelated totals.</p></section><section class="road-step"><h2><span>6</span>The appraisers negotiate line by line</h2><p>This is where most appraisals actually settle. The two appraisers walk through the estimates together, agreeing on what they can and isolating what they can’t. Typical sticking points include:</p><ul><li>Roof quantities, waste factors and steep or high-roof charges</li><li>Whether undamaged materials must be replaced so repairs match</li><li>Code upgrades, overhead and profit, and depreciation rates</li><li>Unit prices for local labor and materials after a big storm</li></ul><p>When the two appraisers sign an agreed figure, that agreement of any two usually sets the amount of loss, and the umpire may never be needed. Talks can take a few weeks or a few months depending on the size of the loss and how far apart the estimates started.</p></section><section class="road-step"><h2><span>7</span>Umpire review of open items</h2><p>If items remain open, the appraisers submit them to the umpire. Each side typically sends its estimate, photos, measurements and a short written position on each disputed line. The umpire may inspect the property, ask questions or request more documents.</p><p>The umpire then decides the disputed items, and an award signed by the umpire and either appraiser generally becomes binding on the amount of loss under most clauses. The umpire doesn’t rule on coverage, bad faith or policy interpretation. Those questions stay with the parties and, if needed, the courts.</p><p>Keep your position organized by line item. A clear spreadsheet and labeled photos make it easier for an umpire to follow your reasoning.</p></section><section class="road-step"><h2><span>8</span>The award, payment and holdback</h2><p>The signed award lists the amount of loss, often split into replacement cost value and actual cash value by coverage. The carrier then applies the deductible, subtracts prior payments and issues payment under the policy’s terms, which may name a set number of days after the award. Mortgage companies are often listed on the check.</p><p>If your policy pays actual cash value first, the depreciation held back is usually recoverable once repairs are finished and documented, within the time your policy allows. Track that deadline closely.</p><p>Use our <a href="/claim-calculator-suite/holdback-recovery-calculator/">holdback recovery calculator</a> to estimate what remains after the first check. If payment stalls after an award, talk to a Mississippi attorney about your options.</p></section></div><h2>Roadmap questions</h2><div class="faqs"><details><summary>How long does a Mississippi appraisal usually take?</summary><p>Many residential appraisals wrap up in one to four months, but there is no fixed timeline. The size of the loss, how far apart the estimates are, and whether an umpire is needed all affect the schedule.</p></details><details><summary>Can my carrier refuse to take part in appraisal?</summary><p>If your policy has an appraisal clause, either side can usually invoke it, but carriers sometimes object that the dispute is about coverage rather than amount. If your carrier refuses or stalls, talk to a Mississippi attorney about your options.</p></details><details><summary>Is an appraisal award final?</summary><p>Under most clauses an award signed by any two of the three panel members sets the amount of loss and is generally binding on that question. Coverage issues, deductibles and prior payments are still applied after the award, and limited challenges may exist, so ask a Mississippi attorney about your specific case.</p></details></div></article><aside class="side"><div class="side-in"><div class="side-box side-call"><h2 class="side-h">Talk it through first</h2><p>Free first conversation. We’ll tell you plainly whether appraisal fits your claim.</p><a class="side-phone" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> +1 (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-gold btn-block" href="/talk-to-an-appraiser/">Send claim details</a><small>Mon–Fri 8am–6pm CT</small></div><div class="side-box"><h2 class="side-h">Resources</h2><ul class="side-links"><li><a href="/appraisal-roadmap/" aria-current="page">Appraisal roadmap</a></li><li><a href="/questions-answered/">Questions answered</a></li><li><a href="/insurance-claim-terms/">Insurance claim terms</a></li><li><a href="/claim-advice/">Claim advice</a></li></ul></div></div></aside></div><section class="band"><div class="wrap band-in rv"><div><h2>Not sure the carrier’s number is right?</h2><p>Send the estimate and a few photos. We’ll review it and tell you what we see — no obligation.</p></div><div class="btns"><a class="btn btn-gold" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/talk-to-an-appraiser/">Email the estimate</a></div></div></section>
